---
import ToolPage from '../layouts/ToolPage.astro';
import Watermark from '../tools/Watermark';
import { getTool } from '../data/tools';

const tool = getTool('add-watermark');
---

<ToolPage tool={tool}>
  <Watermark slot="tool" client:load />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="placement">Where to put a watermark</h2>
      <p>
        Every placement is a trade between how hard it is to remove and how much it interferes
        with the image. There is no setting that is good at both.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Placement</th>
              <th>Removal difficulty</th>
              <th>Intrusiveness</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Corner, low opacity</td>
              <td>Low — a crop removes it in seconds</td>
              <td>Minimal</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Centred, very low opacity</td>
              <td>Moderate — needs deliberate retouching</td>
              <td>Noticeable</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Tiled across the image</td>
              <td>High — covers everything worth taking</td>
              <td>Significant</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Most people default to a corner because it looks professional, then are surprised when a
        cropped copy appears elsewhere. Decide honestly how much you mind: if the answer is "not
        very", a corner mark is fine and mostly serves as attribution. If the answer is "a great
        deal", tile it and accept the cost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="opacity">Getting the opacity right</h2>
      <p>
        Between 25 and 50 percent works for most photographs. Below 20 percent a watermark
        disappears entirely against busy or bright areas; above 60 percent it starts to compete
        with the image.
      </p>
      <p>
        The harder problem is that one setting has to work across a whole batch. White text at 35
        percent looks right on a dark photo and vanishes on a bright one. Two things help: turn on
        the drop shadow, which keeps light text readable over light areas at almost no visual
        cost, and preview against your brightest and darkest images before committing to a large
        batch.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="marketplaces">Watermarks and marketplace listings</h2>
      <p>
        This is worth checking before you watermark a batch of product photos, because the rules
        differ and one of them is strict.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Amazon does not allow them.</strong> Watermarks, logos and added text on the main
        product image are against Amazon's image requirements, and listings can be suppressed for
        it. The main image must be the product on a pure white background and nothing else.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Etsy and eBay permit them</strong>, but plenty of experienced sellers avoid them
        anyway — a watermark reads as defensive, and it interrupts the clean product photography
        that converts best.
      </p>
      <p>
        Watermarking makes far more sense on blog images, portfolio work, photography you licence,
        and social posts, where attribution has real value and there is no platform rule against
        it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="logo-tips">Using a logo instead of text</h2>
      <p>
        Upload a PNG with a genuinely transparent background. A logo saved on a solid white
        rectangle will show that rectangle sitting on top of your photograph, which is the most
        common mistake here.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is worth keeping two versions — one light, one dark — and switching depending on the
        image. A dark logo on a dark photograph is invisible no matter what opacity you choose.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your logo needs preparing first, the <a href="/compress-png/">PNG tools</a> will keep
        the transparency intact, and the <a href="/resize-image/">resizer</a> can bring an
        oversized logo down to a sensible working size.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2 id="honesty">What a watermark actually protects</h2>
      <p>
        Not much, and it is better to know that going in. A watermark is a deterrent and an
        attribution mark, not a security measure. Corner marks are cropped off in seconds,
        centred ones can be retouched, and automated removal tools have become genuinely good at
        both.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it does reliably is make casual reuse slightly inconvenient and make the source
        obvious when an image is shared onward intact. That is worth something. Just keep your
        unwatermarked originals, because the watermark is burned into the output permanently and
        there is no undo.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
